# Copy to `android/key.properties` and fill in. That copy is NOT in the
# repository, and neither is the keystore it points at — see `.gitignore`.
#
# WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS. Android decides whether an update may
# replace an installed app by comparing signatures. Whoever holds this keystore
# can publish something that overwrites Archlence on a user's phone; and if it
# is lost, no future version can ever update an already-installed one. There is
# no recovery for either. Back it up somewhere that is not this machine, and
# not beside the source.
#
# Make one (validity in days — 10000 is a little over 27 years, which is what
# Play wants; a certificate that expires strands the app):
#
#   keytool -genkey -v \
#     -keystore ~/archlence-release.jks \
#     -keyalg RSA -keysize 2048 -validity 10000 \
#     -alias archlence
#
# No `-storetype`: JDK 9 and later default to PKCS12, and asking for the old
# JKS format only earns a warning telling you to migrate. One consequence —
# a PKCS12 store has ONE password, so `storePassword` and `keyPassword` below
# are the same value.
#
# It will ask for that password and for a name and organisation. The password
# is yours; nothing in this repository should ever contain it.
#
# Then check what you made, and keep the fingerprint somewhere:
#
#   keytool -list -v -keystore ~/archlence-release.jks -alias archlence

# An absolute path, or one relative to `android/app/`.
storeFile=/home/you/archlence-release.jks
storePassword=
keyAlias=archlence
keyPassword=
